        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill for long distance running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For high weekly mileage, a non-folding machine with a strong motor and a 22 x 60 in. deck, such as the Sole TT8. If you need a folding treadmill, the Sole F83 and Horizon 7.4 AT both have 22 in. belts and 3.5 HP motors. On a tighter budget, the Sole F65 has a 60 in. deck and a 3.25 HP motor.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill for triathlon training?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              One with a continuous-duty motor, a 60 in. deck, quick speed controls for brick runs, and app connectivity that does not depend on a subscription. The Horizon 7.4 AT meets all four. Position it next to your bike trainer so you can move from bike to run safely.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What motor do you need for long runs on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A continuous-duty rating of at least 3.0 CHP, and 3.5 CHP or more for heavier runners or very long sessions. A motor running near its limit for two hours runs hot, so headroom matters more for long runs than for short ones. Check that the listed figure is continuous, not peak.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can a treadmill handle marathon-length runs?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A well-built one can, provided it has a continuous-duty motor with headroom, a 60 in. deck, and space around it for airflow. Keep the motor compartment clean and the deck lubricated as the maker specifies, and stop if the machine smells hot or cuts out.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a 20 inch treadmill belt wide enough for long distance running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For many runners, yes, but 22 in. is more comfortable on long runs. As you tire, your stride wanders, and a wider belt leaves more margin. Heavier runners and those with a wide or uneven gait benefit most from 22 in.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do you stay cool on long treadmill runs?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Put a standing or wall fan in front of the treadmill, aimed at your upper body; built-in console fans help only a little. Keep the room ventilated, drink to thirst, and avoid running long in a hot garage. Indoors there is no headwind, so you heat up faster than outside.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

